          "filename": "Tin Hau Temple, Yau Ma Tei 油麻地 天后廟 (5282296030).jpg",
          "caption": "Tin Hau (Heavenly Empress) is a woman who actually had lived somewhere along the South China Sea coast, reputed to have had supernatural powers. She is a key deity in Taoism.\nIn the Chinese religious traditions, Guanyin, the Buddhist Goddess of Mercy, is also revered in Taoism. Guanyin is known to take many different shapes - male, female, child, or animal - to approach those who call her name; here in southern coastal China, Tin Hau is considered to be one of those mortal manifestations of Guanyin.\nThere are dozens of Taoist temples in Hong Kong dedicated to Tin Hau; I am visiting one of the better known ones, the one smack in the middle of Kowloon, in Yau Ma Tei district, only a short walk from my hotel.\n\nThis banner is certainly alluding to Guanyin, whose name is pronounced as Kwun Yam in Cantonese.",
